WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
The argument that feminism is no longer needed in the U.S. has been increasing in prevalence,

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.160
so let's talk about why the feminist fight is not over. This argument stems from the idea that now

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.680
in the U.S. men and women are equal under the law, and so there's nothing left for feminists to be

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.440
fighting for. And it is true that after the passage of the 19th amendment and other acts

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.160
fighting for gender equality, that the feminist movement no longer revolves around fighting for

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.720
legislative changes the way that it once did. With the advancement of legal equality in the U.S.,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.800
the feminist movement has not disappeared, it's just evolved. Today's challenges are mostly

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.720
cultural, social, and revolve around how existing laws are implemented.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.140
In other words, double standards and patriarchal traditions are still deeply

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.260
rooted in our culture and it's important to raise awareness about it.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.700
With this being said, these changes to the feminist movement do complicate the

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.500
movement because what success looks like is much less tangible.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.700
But feminism is definitely still needed. Women are still

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.460
underrepresented in leadership, victims to gender-based violence and gender pay

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.460
gaps, and don't have full access to

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.540
reproductive rights. Double standards, harsh media scrutiny,

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:04.060
and unattainable societal standards still hold women back today and that's why feminism is still relevant.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.900
That's all she wrote, bye guys!
